    "product_id": "793b53f4-3276-44ff-92a2-d64650ee0bce",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 260508\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1008 PM MST Sun Oct 25 2026\n\n.UPDATE...Updated 06z Aviation Discussion...\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Temperatures for the upcoming week will generally be within the\n  normal range for late October with lower desert highs mostly in\n  the mid eighties.\n\n- Dry and tranquil weather conditions will last through the middle\n  of week.\n\n- A return to inclement weather with increased rainfall chances is\n  likely towards the end of the week. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nThe center of the low continues to linger across Northern \nArizona, but behind the low, as is continues to track to the NE, \ndrier and warmer conditions are expected for the lower desert. On\nthe RAP40 500MB analysis ridging can already be seen building \nover the coastline of southern CA and stretching into the Intermountain\nWest. However the increasing H5 heights will be somewhat \nsuppressed as another upper level low, currently positioned just \noff the coast of Washington, will push inland starting early \nMonday. This low is expected to stay in northern CONUS, and not \ndive south towards the Desert SW, however as mentioned before this\nwill help suppress H5 height aloft from building over the lower\ndeserts, resulting in near normal heights for this time of year \nand quasi-zonal westerly dry flow lasting through Wednesday. \n\nWith that being said temperatures will be in the low to mid 80s\ntoday, then starting tomorrow increase to the mid to upper 80s,\nlasting through Wednesday. NBM forecast highs do show readings \nnudging more toward the upper 80s for Tuesday and Wednesday, but \nthere is currently only a 25% chance (for KPHX) at reaching 90 \ndegrees this week. Meanwhile morning lows will be in the low to \nmid 60s. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /THURSDAY THROUGH NEXT SUNDAY/... \nModels continue to suggest a more organized large scale troughing\npattern setting up later this week with the parent low setting up\nalong the British Colombia coast before diving southward into the\nPacific Northwest by Thursday. A secondary shortwave or weak\nclosed low may also form west of California Wednesday into\nThursday, more associated with the sub-tropical jet. What is \nlikely to be more important is the eventual phasing of the polar \njet over the Pacific Northwest with the stronger subtropical jet \nby next Friday. Both the EPS and GEFS support this phasing, while \nalso strengthening the subtropical jet to near record strength \nfor this time of year just west of Baja on Friday. Guidance then \nsupports a fairly strong weather system (with H5 heights within \nthe 10th percentile of climatology) developing within the base of\nthe trough by next weekend. Although we are at around 5-7 days \nout from this next expected weather event, there is enough model \nevidence for us to begin worrying about another significant \nprecipitation event. A bit more than 50% of the ensemble members \nfavor the trough deepening just off the West Coast Friday into \nnext weekend, likely pushing a modest to strong atmospheric river \n(AR) somewhere into the Southwestern U.S. next weekend.\n\nAs the trough first begins to set up during the middle part of the\nweek, there is high confidence in strong northward moisture \nadvection in the upper levels out of Mexico Wednesday night into\nThursday. This should be followed by lower level moisture \nadvection later Thursday into Friday. Although the timing may\nchange and assuming the positioning of the subtropical jet is\ncorrect, shower chances may begin as early as late Thursday and \ncarry into Friday under broad divergence aloft and modest upslope \nsoutherly flow. This first period of rain chances very well could \nchange and confidence is still quite low in how much rain could \nfall.\n\nScattered mostly high terrain rain chances may continue into \nSaturday before the main weather system traverses the region at \nsome point Saturday night and Sunday. Additional moisture \nadvection is likely to occur, associated with the AR, right ahead\nof the weekend system, but it is too early to know where exactly \nthe best moisture and forcing will end up. Even if we do not get a\ndirect hit, ensemble guidance is heavily favoring at least a \npartial hit from the AR late Saturday into Sunday. Preliminary QPF\nnumbers show upwards of an inch may be possible over the south- \ncentral Arizona lower deserts to upwards of 2\" possible in the \nhigh terrain north and east of Phoenix. These numbers very well \ncould change and seem to lean more on the optimistic side. If the \nupper end of precip guidance comes to fruition, locations across \ncentral Arizona are expected to have to worry about rising stream\nand river levels again, but it is doubtful there will be big \nflood concern due to the longer duration of the event. Given we \nare still outside the winter season, this weather system will be \non the warmer side with snow levels likely staying above 9-10K \nfeet. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 0505Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nNo aviation weather concerns expected throughout the TAF period.\nThe overall wind pattern will exhibit the typical diurnal\ntendencies with overall speeds aob 7 kts along with extended\nperiods of light variability to even calm conditions. Mostly \nclear skies will give way to some passing high clouds during the \nday Monday.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nNo aviation weather concerns expected throughout the TAF period.\nWind speeds will generally be light aob 6 kts with a westerly\ncomponent favored at KIPL and a northwest to northeasterly\ncomponent favored at KBLH. Extended periods of light variability\nto even calm conditions can be expected. Clear skies will give way\nto some passing high clouds during the overnight and morning \nhours.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nHigh pressure will settle back into the region over the next\ncouple of days leading to drier conditions and temperatures\nreturning into the normal range. Minimum humidity levels will \nfall into a 20-40% range this afternoon and then 15-35% on \nMonday. Overnight recoveries will remain good at 45-70% across the\narea. Winds will remain light each day favoring typical diurnal\npatterns. A shift in the weather pattern late this week and into \nnext weekend may bring about cooler, wetter, and breezier \nconditions.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Ryan/Kuhlman\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman\nAVIATION...Lojero\nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman\n",
